  {
    "type": "updateEntity",
    "id": {
      "$bytes": "fa143b7aa6e4406f92f557bec456995c"
    },
    "set": [
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "a126ca530c8e48d5b88882c734c38935"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "School-finance reform estimates: methods and coverage",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "e3e363d1dd294ccb8e6ff3b76d99bc33"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "## Coverage\n\nThis initial publication contains one all-sample education-years coefficient. The remaining 14 selected estimates and cost-model reconciliation are not yet published.\n\n## Study and exposure\n\nThe study links school finance reforms and district spending with Panel Study of Income Dynamics birth cohorts from 1955 through 1985, followed through 2011. The preferred instrumental-variable specification uses court-ordered reform exposure and predicted spending-change quartiles. These estimates depend on the study's identification assumptions; they are not randomized trial effects.\n\n## Reading the coefficients\n\nThe exposure is the natural log of average school-age per-pupil spending. Tables III–V supply coefficients in years, probability units or log-income units per unit of log spending. The authors' approximate interpretation for a 10% sustained spending increase uses a 0.10 change; an exact 10% increase would use ln(1.10). Neither should silently replace the reported coefficients.\n\n## Population and uncertainty\n\nLow-income means parental family income below twice the poverty line in any childhood year. Nonpoor is the study's complementary group, not a synonym for never below the official poverty line. Standard errors are clustered by school district. Model counts combine income groups and are not subgroup-specific sample sizes.\n\n## Cost illustration\n\nThe article's illustrative benefit-cost discussion is distinct from the regressions. Its rounded figures and differing wage assumptions require reconciliation before a computed cost-effectiveness comparison. National spending exposure is not a uniform program price.\n\n## Source\n\n[Final journal article](https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjv036), Tables III–V and printed pp. 165, 212–213. [Author-hosted full text](https://cpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/sites.northwestern.edu/dist/b/3664/files/2020/10/QJE_resubmit_final_version_stamped.pdf).\n\n## Publication\n\nC. Kirabo Jackson; Rucker C. Johnson; Claudia Persico. Quarterly Journal of Economics 131 (2016), 157–218. DOI 10.1093/qje/qjv036. The working-paper version has different headline values.\n\n## Sample counts for the first row\n\nTable III reports 15,353 individuals and 4,586 childhood families for the model. These are not subgroup-specific counts.",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "unset": []
  },
